      "original_en": "**Direct answer:** The AD-Alterome evidence links oxidative stress to mitochondrial dysfunction mainly through shared pathological cascades and gene-level mechanisms, but most records are associative or model-based rather than causal human proof.\n\n**Supporting points**\n\n- Mitochondrial dysfunction in AD is described as arising alongside Aβ accumulation, tau tangles, and oxidative stress, indicating these processes co-occur in disease pathogenesis (PMID 35950245).\n- A mechanistic hypothesis: impaired AD-associated mutant TOMM40 (F113L, F131L) is proposed to cause mitochondrial Aβ accumulation, mitochondrial malfunction, and microglial oxidative stress, leading to NLRP3 inflammasome activation and neuronal death (PMID 36835494). This is explicitly a hypothesis, not demonstrated causation.\n- In an APP mutant transgenic mouse model, increased mitochondrial gene expression and oxidative damage were reported, supporting a mitochondria–oxidative damage link in an animal model (PMID 25531259).\n- In non-AD-specific models, LDL receptor knockout mice showed brain oxidative stress together with mitochondrial dysfunction (PMID 38056504); TMEM97 knockout in RPE cells exacerbated oxidative stress and destabilized mitochondria and lysosomes, with organelle dysfunction elevating ROS (PMID 34245862). These are not AD experiments.\n- Human postmortem AD brain samples with the common TREM2 variant showed elevated oxidative-stress-related and lipid-metabolism genes alongside impaired myelination (PMID 36605616), an association in human tissue.\n- Genetic/oxidative links: trisomy 21 SOD1 overexpression increases oxidative stress in Down syndrome, which carries high AD risk (PMID 31582732); TNIP1 variants may affect the oxidative stress response via altered GPX3 (PMID 38809917); the UNC5C T835M AD-associated mutation leads to neurodegeneration involving oxidative stress in aged mice (PMID 40468412).\n\n**Limitations**\n\n- Most evidence is correlative or from cell/animal models; direct human causal evidence linking oxidative stress to mitochondrial dysfunction in AD is limited.\n- Several records (e.g., PMID 38056504, 34245862, 39877004) lack explicit AD context; database AD labels do not establish an AD experiment.\n- The TOMM40 mechanism is stated as a hypothesis, not confirmed.\n- This synthesis is based on a bounded AD-Alterome candidate pool with lexical screening only; it does not establish full literature coverage or expert-verified causality.",
      "original_zh": "**直接回答：** AD-Alterome证据主要通过共享的病理级联和基因水平机制将氧化应激与线粒体功能障碍联系起来，但大多数记录是关联性或基于模型的，而非人类因果证明。\n\n**支持要点**\n\n- AD中的线粒体功能障碍被描述为与Aβ积累、tau缠结和氧化应激一同出现，表明这些过程在疾病发病机制中共存（PMID 35950245）。\n\n- 一个机制假说：AD相关突变体TOMM40（F113L、F131L）功能受损被提出会导致线粒体Aβ积累、线粒体功能障碍和小胶质细胞氧化应激，从而导致NLRP3炎症小体激活和神经元死亡（PMID 36835494）。这明确是一个假说，而非已证明的因果关系。\n\n- 在APP突变转基因小鼠模型中，报道了线粒体基因表达增加和氧化损伤，支持动物模型中的线粒体–氧化损伤联系（PMID 25531259）。\n\n- 在非AD特异性模型中，LDL受体敲除小鼠表现出脑氧化应激以及线粒体功能障碍（PMID 38056504）；RPE细胞中TMEM97敲除加剧了氧化应激并使线粒体和溶酶体不稳定，细胞器功能障碍升高了ROS（PMID 34245862）。这些不是AD实验。\n\n- 携带常见TREM2变异的人类死后AD脑样本显示氧化应激相关和脂质代谢基因升高，同时伴有髓鞘形成受损（PMID 36605616），这是人类组织中的关联。\n\n- 遗传/氧化联系：21三体SOD1过表达增加了唐氏综合征中的氧化应激，而唐氏综合征具有高AD风险（PMID 31582732）；TNIP1变异可能通过改变GPX3影响氧化应激反应（PMID 38809917）；UNC5C T835M AD相关突变导致老年小鼠中涉及氧化应激的神经退行性变（PMID 40468412）。\n\n**局限性**\n\n- 大多数证据是相关性或来自细胞/动物模型；直接将氧化应激与AD中线粒体功能障碍联系起来的人类因果证据有限。\n\n- 若干记录（例如PMID 38056504、34245862、39877004）缺乏明确的AD背景；数据库AD标签并不能确立AD实验。\n\n- TOMM40机制被表述为假说，而非已确认。\n\n- 本综合基于一个有限的AD-Alterome候选池，仅进行词汇筛选；它并未确立完整的文献覆盖或专家验证的因果关系。\n"
